# POINT 6: System hardening (sysctl, unused services, mount options, AppArmor)

## 1. Security rationale

Points 3 to 5 secured access and the network path. This point reduces what the system itself exposes if something gets in, and makes post-compromise movement harder.

| Area | Why it matters |
|---|---|
| **Kernel parameters (sysctl)** | Turn off network behaviours a server never needs (redirects, source routing, forwarding), and restrict kernel information leaks that help exploit writers (`kptr_restrict`, `dmesg_restrict`, ptrace limits). |
| **Disabled kernel modules** | Rarely used protocols and filesystems have a long history of kernel vulnerabilities. If the module can't load, the bug can't be reached. |
| **Unused services** | Every running service is code to patch and a potential listener. Fewer services means a smaller attack surface. |
| **Mount options** | `nodev`, `nosuid` and `noexec` stop an attacker who can write to `/tmp` or `/var/log` from turning that into code execution or privilege escalation. This pays off the partition layout we chose in Point 1. |
| **AppArmor** | Mandatory access control: even a compromised service is confined to what its profile allows. |

**Scope:** the full set of changes goes on the **Target**. On the **Bastion** we apply only the sysctl file, the module blacklist, the service inventory and the AppArmor check. Its default single-partition layout doesn't support per-mount options.

**No NAT window is needed in this point.** Everything below uses packages already installed.

**Safety net:** the `P5-firewall` snapshot is your rollback. The only change that can stop the machine from booting is `/etc/fstab`, so we validate it before rebooting.

